first_n_text:
 Every connected graph without loops or multiple edges is hamiltonian-connected, i.e., it has a system of cycles that includes every vertex, if it has at least three vertices and either:

- it is bipartite;
- its chromatic number is equal to its degree;
- it does not contain an induced path of odd length longer than three.

 We find necessary and sufficient conditions for a graph to be almost hypohamiltonian, i.e., to be as close as possible to hypohamiltonian without actually being so. These conditions are in terms of the existence of certain walks in the graph. The main tools are a potential function for almost hypohamiltonian graphs and a new notion of incompatible pairs of vertices.

 This paper is a continuation of our previous work, where we studied hypohamiltonian graphs, i.e., graphs that are as close as possible to being hamiltonian without actually being so. In that work, we found necessary and sufficient conditions for a graph to be hypohamiltonian in terms of the existence of certain cycles in the graph.

We introduce the notion of graded comodule categories with enough projectives, a generalization of the usual notion of comodule categories with enough injectives. We show that graded comodule categories with enough projectives are characterized by a universal property, and we develop some of their basic theory, including a definition of graded quotients and a description of their homotopy theory. We also show that the category of graded comodules over a graded coalgebra is a graded comodule category with enough projectives, and we use this result to describe the homotopy theory of graded coalgebras.

This paper is a continuation of [arxiv.org/abs/1507.00819].

[Graded comodule categories with enough projectives]{}

[Michael Batanin]{}
